Michal Jaegermann wrote:

> It may be.  Your machine should still have a "fail-safe monitor".
> Usually to turn that on you need to switch postition of some jumpers
> on a motherboard.  Which ones this is a particular mobo specific.
> So, unless somebody with the same board will tell you details of
> the procedure, you have to dig out (try Google) manuals for your
> mobo.  There should be also a description how to reload a firmware
> from fail-safe when a disaster, like the one you are describing,
> strikes.

First I spend some hours googling after some documents for my alpha computer
's motherboard but failed in the end. I know it is from the Force company
(www.forcecomputers.com) but I found no link on their page for a detailed
motherboard technical document.

So I opened the computer case (again) and looked for some prints on the
platine but found only jumper enumerations, no describing hint.
But I noticed that last time that I opened the case (for cleaning the
inventory) I missed to plug a serial cable from the cpu daughterboard to
the back plane. Now that the cable is connected again, the codes received
with the serial connection is different. Now the codes doesn't repeat as
before, or let's say they have now another pattern ;-). I also noticed that
the speed with which the characters are printed decreased considerable. The
attached minicom capture was produced within some 3 - 4 hours where I let
the alpha run, within the whole time the monitor never got a signal.

If I understand the documents from the web correctly, then there seems to be
a problem with the SROM so that the firmware never gets loaded at all.

matthias


